Found while fixing #13.

lib/Git/Libgit2/FFI.pm, the =func blocks for git_cherrypick_commit (~line
2018) and git_revert_commit (~line 2038):

    Git::Libgit2::FFI::git_cherrypick_commit(\my $oid, $repo, $cherrypick, $our_commit, $parent_count, $opts);

    Create the actual cherry-pick commit.

Three things are wrong, and they are the same three things that made the
test in #13 meaningless:

1. arg0 is git_index **out (confirmed against /usr/include/git2/cherrypick.h
   and revert.h, libgit2 1.5.1) -- the merged index, not an OID. Naming it
   $oid tells the caller to hand over a git_oid buffer.
2. Neither function creates a commit. They compute the index that results
   from applying (or inverting) the change. "Create the actual cherry-pick
   commit" is the opposite of what happens.
3. The returned handle is owned by the caller and must be released with
   git_index_free. Nothing says so, and this distribution has no DESTROY.

Also: the 5th parameter is documented as $parent_count but the header calls
it `unsigned int mainline` -- the parent index to diff a merge commit
against, which must be 0 for a non-merge commit. Passing 1 fails with
"mainline branch specified but <oid> is not a merge commit"; t/34 now
asserts exactly that.

Fix is prose-only in FFI.pm; the binding itself is correct
([ "opaque*", "git_repository", "git_commit", "git_commit", "uint", "opaque" ]).
Worth a sweep of the neighbouring =func blocks for the same wrong-out-param
wording while in there.
